# rp2040_geek

A Waveshare RP2040-GEEK: an RP2040 with 4MB of flash in a USB-stick case,
carrying a 1.14 inch 240 by 135 IPS panel on an ST7789 over four-wire SPI and a
TF card socket, with its native USB port on a USB-A plug.

It derives from pico because that is what it is: the RP2040 the Pico SDK
configures as pico. The SDK does publish a waveshare_rp2040_geek header, but
the bridge's vendor vocabulary is the six Raspberry Pi boards, and deriving
from pico and correcting its defaults in the board table is what every other
composite board here does. The flash beyond pico's 2MB goes unused.

The panel is on SPI1: DC GP8, CS GP9, clock GP10, data GP11, reset GP12, and
the backlight on GP25 through an NPN switch, so high is on. The TF socket is
wired for four-bit SDIO on GP18 to GP23 -- clock GP18, command GP19, data GP20
to GP23 -- which in SPI mode is SPI0 with clock GP18, MOSI GP19, MISO GP20,
and CS GP23. The four-pin I2C header is GP28 and GP29, I2C0's SDA and SCL;
the three-pin header labelled UART is GP4 and GP5, UART1's TX and RX; and the
three-pin header labelled SWD is plain GPIO, GP2 and GP3, which debug-probe
firmware drives as SWCLK and SWDIO and the SDK's header names I2C1.

Both three-pin headers follow the Raspberry Pi Debug Probe's ports: the same
three-pin JST-SH connector in the same order, TX, GND, RX on the UART header
and SWCLK, GND, SWDIO on the SWD header, so the probe's cables fit them
directly. A Debug Probe's UART port on the UART header is this board's
console, because the board table makes UART1 on GP4 and GP5 the default UART.
The SWD header is the probe's side of that link, not the target's: the
schematic wires it to GP2 and GP3, not to the RP2040's SWCLK and SWDIO, which
reach no header or test point. It is what the board drives when it runs
debug-probe firmware itself. The openocd runner and debugger accept this
board, since it inherits pico's rp2040 machine, but they need a probe on the
chip's own debug port, which this schematic does not bring out.

Two of pico's defaults are wrong here, and the Pico bridge's board table
replaces them: GP25 is the backlight, not an LED, so the board reports no LED,
and the default UART's GP0 and GP1 go nowhere, so the default UART is UART1 on
the exposed GP4 and GP5, as the SDK's own header has it. ADC_AVDD is the 3V3
rail, so the ADC reference is 3300 mV; of the four ADC inputs only GP28 and
GP29, the I2C header's pins, reach the outside.

Every pin above was read from Waveshare's schematic,
RP2040-GEEK-Schematic.pdf. The SDK's header names GP17 as the SD card's chip
select, which the schematic does not support: GP17 is unconnected and the card's
CD/D3 is GP23. None of it is qualified on hardware.

https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/RP2040-GEEK
